## C64 — the C-II response defect is PSD and strict at linearly many terminals

For the relative-interior fully force-visible KKT stress, let `Lambda` be the positive-contact response on the loaded force-hull boundary `B`, and `L_H` the positive-diameter Laplacian.

C53 descends through the contact Schur complement and gives

`M=Lambda-L_H >= 0`.

Therefore every boundary cut is response-dominated:

`sum_(b in S,k notin S) c_bk >= sum_(b in S,k notin S) beta_bk`.

In particular the response degree `d_b` at each loaded terminal dominates its diameter-load degree `s_b`.

If equality holds at one terminal, `M_bb=0`; PSD then forces the whole row `M_b*=0`. Thus

`c_bk=beta_bk` for every `k`.

A zero-excess terminal has no direct loaded-loaded unit contact, and every harmonic component touching it attaches all other boundary pins at diameter distance from it.

Each harmonic component contains at most one zero-excess terminal: if two zero terminals shared a component, the component's strict response clique and row equality would make three attachments pairwise diametral, contradicting the large-n anti-Reuleaux theorem.

Every zero terminal must be incident to some harmonic component. Such a component has at least three attachments, hence at least two strict-excess attachments. A strict terminal belongs to at most three harmonic attachment sets because its contact degree is at most three. Double counting gives

`2|Z| <= 3|T|`,

so

`|T| >= 2|B|/5 >= (ceil D+2)/5`.

Thus the strict C41 response defect is distributed over `Omega(D)` loaded terminals; it cannot be localized at finitely many boundary exceptions.

## Next target

The remaining issue is magnitude, not support. Convert the linear strict-terminal set into a lower bound on

`sum_b (d_b-s_b) = tr(Lambda-L_H)`

or directly into short-pair response mass. Candidate inputs are the uniform C43 geometric deficit, the C40 total attachment-diameter budget, and the unit-contact mechanical equilibrium that the abstract near-star regression omits.
